// LH-2 negative: a bare arm body must be CHECKED, not skipped.
//
// `nope` is declared nowhere. Before this fix the checker walked only Block arm
// bodies, so this ExprStmt body was never examined and the program was
// accepted -- the arm was never looked at. K102 is emitted from exactly one
// place (checkExpr's identifier case), so a skipped body could never report it.
//
// The gate for this fixture therefore asserts the CODE and the NAME, not merely
// a non-zero exit: that is what distinguishes "checked and rejected" from
// "never examined". A gate that only checked the exit status would pass on the
// unfixed checker, because that checker accepts the program.
//
// Statement position on purpose: the value of a bare arm is not what is under
// test here, and keeping the match in statement position removes the
// expression-position dependency from the negative case.
func f(x) {
    if (x > 0) { return Ok(42) }
    return Err("bad")
}

func main() {
    let r = f(1)
    match r {
        Ok(n) => nope,
        Err(e) => 0,
    }
}